[61] GEN 2

[62] "Gen 2" masks are another popular mask type and were used in Scream 2 (1997). Gen 2 is not a "second generation" of molds, but a nickname given by fans during a time early masks were incorrectly seen as a binary. They have heart shaped nose paint and appear somewhat long faced with pushed out nose bridges, squared jaws, and larger eyes. Like HNs, the bloating is likely a result of how the master was mounted during the molding process. As former Fun World designer Brigitte Sleiertin-Linden tells us "when the company increased production to meet demand after the mask got popular, they had multiple factories making the same item". The earliest we may be able to date Gen 2s is currently 1996. Gen 2s for all of the ghost characters became the primary production for the #9206 and #9207 assortments in 1997 on.

[63] UPPER DIMPLE

[64] CHEEK POCKS

[65] TEXTURED MOUTH

[66] DOT

[67] A Gen 2 mask may feature one of these defects (not multiple or all).

[68] Though we've recognized a few markings to identify Gen 2 masks, there seem to be many more additional production molds, which makes sense considering the rising popularity the Scream brought the design.

[69] MID-90S

[70] The earliest we can currently date Gen 2s may be 1996. Mid-90s are less common and feature larger paint stencils than their 97 counterparts.

[71] 97

[72] In 1997, Gen 2 masks featured distinctly thin eyeliner and feature recognizable heart-shaped nose. Masks nicknamed "Scream 2-style" are 97 masks with characteristics similar to those from Scream 2 (1997), such as wider cut jaws that expose the perimeter line.

[73] POLY

[74] Gen 2 molds saw their final use with polyester shrouds and [vanilla scent](https://www.itsfuntyme.com/post/vanilla-or-phthalates). We believe the transition to polyester occurred in late 1997.

[75] Cotton shroud Gen 2 masks were packaged with a pink 'party orb' style header tag labeled Fantastic Faces.

[76] Polyester shroud Gen 2s were packaged with three different header cards including Fearsome Faces, purple ASIS (As Seen in Scream), and black ASIS.

[77] Example of exposed perimeter lines on "Scream 2 style" masks. The perimeter line is present on all mask types but are usually covered by the shroud. We believe this line was used as a guide to cut out a raw casting.

[78] On Gen 2s, visible remnants of the previous perimeter line are covered and moved about a half inch.

[79] Gen 2 molds were made to increase production of all Fun World's ghost characters in the #9206 assortment. They also exhibit a similar long face and stuffed look with deeper jawlines.

[80] Wide jaw on a Gen 2 'Happy' ghost with exposed perimeter lines.

[81] *Gen 2 masks have been found as #9206 Glow-in-Dark Ghost Mask with Shroud (cotton, poly), #9207 Fluorescent Ghost Mask with Shroud (cotton, poly), #9223 All-in-One Ghost Mask & Gown, #9219 Instant Disguise, #8919 Droop Collar Mask, and #9974 Scream Stalker costume masks with bib.

[82] NU-GEN

[83] Fun World is resurrecting the original 'Weeping' ghost in 2026. It will be featured this year on select 30th Anniversary Ghost Face products celebrating Scream (1996). This new mask was created using an original Gen 1— though by what process we do not yet know. The initial launch of the Nu-Gen molds will have a short tasselless shroud and detachable sparkle hood for the #93828 - Ghost Face 30th Anniversary Mask product; and various 30th Anniversary Ghost Face costumes with a movie-style sparkle fabric robe. Though it's future is uncertain, Fun World's RJ Torbert has said they hope to use the new mask type on new non-anniversary products at a later time.

[84] *Nu-Gen masks will be found as #93828 - Ghost Face 30th Anniversary Mask, #136764 - 30th Anniversary Ghost Face® - Adult, #136765 - 30th Anniversary Ghost Face® - Plus, and #136762 - 30th Anniversary Ghost Face® - Child.

